      "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0",
      "artist": "Roger Wollstadt",
      "description": "The Pulteney Bridge was built over the River Avon in Bath, England in 1773.  The original bridge was designed by Robert Adam.  \nPulteney Bridge has been rebuilt and revised a few times, and restored to something like its original condition.  It is now a \"grade I listed building.\"  Like the Ponte Vecchio in Florence, Italy, it has shops on both sides, extending the full length of the span. \nThe bridge was built because a local landowner wanted to build houses on the opposite side of the river.  Apparently property developers are not all bad.  :-)\n\nThis is not the River Avon that goes through Stratford, Shakespeare's birthplace.  Or for that matter, the one that goes through Salisbury, about 30 miies (50 km) away.  There are at least five rivers by that name in England, plus a few in Scotland.",
